hypotheses.statement:
The functions $a,c$ are positive and satisfy, with absolute comparison constants, $$\begin{aligned}
 a(s)+c(s)&=c_0(s),&
 a(s)&\asymp c_0(s),&
 c(s)&\asymp c_0(s)\qquad(s\ge1),\label{eq:functions-deficits}\\
 s a(s)&=\int_{s-1}^{\infty}c(t)\,dt
       &&(s\ge2),\label{eq:functions-future-a}\\
 s c(s)&=\int_{s-1}^{\infty}a(t)\,dt
       &&(s\ge1).\label{eq:functions-future-c}
\end{aligned}$$ For $u\ge2$, $$\label{eq:functions-dickman-ratio}
 \frac1{4u^2}\le\frac{\rho(u)}{\rho(u-1)}\le\frac1u .$$ Moreover, for each fixed $\lambda>0$, there is $U_\lambda$ such that $$\label{eq:functions-dickman-logderivative}
 -\frac{\rho'(u)}{\rho(u)}\ge\lambda\qquad(u\ge U_\lambda).$$

On every fixed compact subset of their respective domains, $\delta_i^{(w)}$ converges uniformly to the continuous function $\delta_i$. Uniformly for sufficiently large $w$, $$\label{eq:ref-anomaly-decay}
 |\delta_i^{(w)}(r)|+|\delta_i(r)|
       \ll\exp(-c r\log(r+2)).$$

Uniformly over all subsets $E$ of the primes less than $P$, $$\label{eq:stops-euler-lower}
 \sum_{\substack{d_0\le P^{.95}\\t\mid d_0\Rightarrow t\in E}}
       \frac{W_E(d_0)}{d_0}
 \ge e^{-\gamma}(.94-o(1)).$$

outputs.exact_statement:
There are absolute constants $c>0$ and $z_0$ with the following property. For every real $z\ge z_0$ and every choice of a residue class $a_p\pmod p$ for each prime $p\le z$, $$\label{eq:main-survivors}
 \#\{1\le n\le Y:n\not\equiv a_p\pmod p\text{ for every }p\le z\}
 \ge c\frac{YV_0}{B^2}.$$

Let $\mathcal P$ be any subset of the primes at most $v$. Suppose that intersection counts for bad conditions indexed by squarefree products $l$ of primes in $\mathcal P$ have the form $$Xg(l)+r_l,$$ where $g$ is multiplicative and $$0\le g(p)\le\frac2p,\qquad g(p)<1,\qquad g(2)\le\frac12
 \quad\text{if }2\in\mathcal P.$$ There are absolute constants $c,C>0$ and $s_0$ such that for $s\ge s_0$ the count avoiding all the bad conditions is $$\label{eq:fundamental-input}
 X\prod_{p\in\mathcal P}(1-g(p))\bigl(1+O(e^{-cs})\bigr)
 +O\!\left(
 \sum_{\substack{l\le v^s\\l\text{ squarefree}\\p\mid l\Rightarrow p\in\mathcal P}}
 \tau(l)^C|r_l|\right).$$ The same statement holds for nonnegative weighted counts. A local condition that makes the sifted set empty may instead be removed from consideration as an exactly empty case.

Let one forbidden class be given at every prime $p\le u$, where $u\to\infty$. Count the points in a progression segment whose coordinate interval has real length $J$, and whose step is coprime to every prime $p\le u$. Put $V_{\mathrm{sm}}(u)=\prod_{p\le u}(1-1/p)$. For each fixed $\eta>0$, uniformly when $J\ge u^\eta$, its sifted count $N$ satisfies $$\label{eq:small-upper}
 N\ll_\eta JV_{\mathrm{sm}}(u).$$ There are absolute $c_1,C_1>0$ and a sufficiently large fixed $v_0$ such that, writing $v=\log_uJ$, uniformly for $v\ge v_0$, $$\label{eq:small-relative}
 \left|\frac{N}{JV_{\mathrm{sm}}(u)}-1\right|\le C_1e^{-c_1v}.$$ All estimates are independent of the progression’s initial point, step and forbidden classes.

The functions $a,c$ are positive and satisfy, with absolute comparison constants, $$\begin{aligned}
 a(s)+c(s)&=c_0(s),&
 a(s)&\asymp c_0(s),&
 c(s)&\asymp c_0(s)\qquad(s\ge1),\label{eq:functions-deficits}\\
 s a(s)&=\int_{s-1}^{\infty}c(t)\,dt
       &&(s\ge2),\label{eq:functions-future-a}\\
 s c(s)&=\int_{s-1}^{\infty}a(t)\,dt
       &&(s\ge1).\label{eq:functions-future-c}
\end{aligned}$$ For $u\ge2$, $$\label{eq:functions-dickman-ratio}
 \frac1{4u^2}\le\frac{\rho(u)}{\rho(u-1)}\le\frac1u .$$ Moreover, for each fixed $\lambda>0$, there is $U_\lambda$ such that $$\label{eq:functions-dickman-logderivative}
 -\frac{\rho'(u)}{\rho(u)}\ge\lambda\qquad(u\ge U_\lambda).$$

The derivative weights are positive and nonincreasing on their indicated ranges. If $i'$ denotes the type opposite to $i$, then $$\label{eq:functions-derivative-integrals}
 \phi_i(s)=\int_{m_i(s)}^{\infty}W_0(t)\phi_{i'}(t)\,dt.$$ This holds also on the specified starting extensions. On a tail, $$\label{eq:functions-phi-dickman}
 \phi_\mathrm e(s)\asymp\rho(s-2),\qquad
 \phi_\mathrm o(s)\asymp\rho(s-2).$$ There are absolute constants $C,q$ such that the absolute logarithmic derivatives, where they exist, and the ratios $\phi_{i'}(m_i(s))/\phi_i(s)$ are at most $C(1+s)^q$. In particular, for $u,v\le S$ in the indicated domain of the same weight, with $S\ge3$, $$|\log\phi_i(u)-\log\phi_i(v)|
 \le C(1+S)^q|u-v|.$$ For each fixed $\lambda>0$, the negative logarithmic derivatives of both weights are at least $\lambda$ on a sufficiently remote tail.

On the ordinary even and odd state spaces, respectively, the finite raw invariant densities are $$\label{eq:functions-invariant-densities}
 \pi_\mathrm e(s)=\phi_\mathrm e(s)\quad(s\ge2),\qquad
 \pi_\mathrm o(s)=(1-s^{-2})\phi_\mathrm o(s)\quad(s\ge1).$$ Put $$\beta=\int_1^3\pi_\mathrm o(s)\,ds=\frac{4A}{3}.$$ For every nonnegative measurable state function $Q$, $$\label{eq:functions-cycle-occupation}
 \mathbb E\sum_{j=1}^{\tau}Q(i_j,s_j)
 =\frac1\beta\sum_i\int Q(i,s)\pi_i(s)\,ds.$$ In particular, if $$M_g=\sum_i\int\pi_i(s)g(s)\,ds,$$ then $\mathbb EG=M_g/\beta$, and $$\label{eq:mg-bound}
 0<M_g\le4e^\gamma.$$

For an admissible compact test and starts satisfying <a href="#eq:functions-start-range" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:functions-start-range">[eq:functions-start-range]</a>, $$\label{eq:functions-occupation-limit}
 \lim_{v\to\infty}
 \mathbb E_{s_{\rm in}}\sum_{n\ge0}F_{i_n}(v-T_n,s_n)
 =
 \frac1{M_g}\sum_i\int_{\mathbb R}\int F_i(u,s)\pi_i(s)\,ds\,du.$$ The convergence is uniform in $s_{\rm in}\in[1.99,2.3]$. The state integrals on the right use the ordinary invariant ranges in <a href="#eq:functions-invariant-densities" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:functions-invariant-densities">[eq:functions-invariant-densities]</a>.

For every fixed $h>0$ there is $C_h<\infty$ with the following properties. For any nonnegative measurable state function $Q$ and any $v\in\mathbb R$, a regenerating start satisfies $$\label{eq:functions-band-from-regeneration}
 \mathbb E\sum_{n\ge1}Q(i_n,s_n)
        \mathbf 1_{\{T_n\in[v,v+h]\}}
 \le C_h\sum_i\int Q(i,s)\pi_i(s)\,ds.$$ From an even start in <a href="#eq:functions-start-range" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:functions-start-range">[eq:functions-start-range]</a>, the same bound holds for the sum over $n\ge2$, uniformly in the starting ratio. The density of the first odd state is at most $$C\,\mathbf 1_{\{s\ge0.98\}}\phi_\mathrm o(s).$$ In particular, uniformly in $r_{\rm in}>0$, $R>0$, and $\ell\in\{1,2\}$, $$\label{eq:functions-inverse-phi-band}
 \mathbb E\sum_{n\ge1}
 \frac{\mathbf 1_{\{R\le r_n\le eR,\ s_n<r_n/\ell\}}}
      {\phi_{i_n}(s_n)}
 \ll R+1.$$ Including the starting state in the last sum changes its bound by at most an absolute constant.

For the killed chain of Proposition <a href="#prop:prime-coupling" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:prime-coupling">17</a>, the probability of a nonexit bin with left endpoint $t$ at step $2\leq n\leq N$ is bounded, with an absolute constant, by $$\label{eq:prime-marginals}
 \begin{cases}
  C h\phi_\mathrm e(t),&n\ \hbox{even},\\[2mm]
  C h\phi_\mathrm o(t)\bigl(1-t^{-2}+3hW_0(t)\bigr),&n\ \hbox{odd}.
 \end{cases}$$ At step $1$ the bound is $C hW_0(t)\phi_\mathrm o(t)$, on the slightly extended range $t\geq0.98$. The constants are uniform in the starting ratios and in $w$ sufficiently large.

The following assertions hold uniformly for $s_{\mathrm{st}}\in[1.99,2.3]$ and $\ell\in\{1,2\}$.*

1.  *For fixed $K$, the original harmonic mass of prefixes with terminal gap at most $K$ is $O_K(B^{-2})$.*


2.  *Consider any selection of such compact-ending prefixes whose low-state histories imply an event $\mathcal A$ in the killed prime chain. Their total weight is at most $$\label{eq:prime-compact-event}
     C_K B^{-2}\mathbb P_w(\mathcal A)+O_{K,A}(B^{-A})$$ for every fixed $A>0$. One may take $\mathcal A$ to be the event that at least one selected compact prefix occurs.*


3.  *For every fixed $c'>0$, $$\label{eq:prime-tail}
     \lim_{K\to\infty}\limsup_{w\to\infty}
     B^2\sum_{\substack{\mathfrak p\ \mathrm{standard}\\r(\mathfrak p)>K}}
           h(\mathfrak p)e^{-c'r(\mathfrak p)}=0.$$ The same compact and tail assertions hold for continuous original harmonic weights.*


4.  *Let $H_i(r,s)$ be bounded continuous functions supported in a fixed compact positive gap interval and a fixed compact ratio interval. Fixed interval boundaries and the indicator $s<r/\ell$ are also permitted, with one-sided continuous extension before imposing that indicator, as in Proposition <a href="#prop:occupation" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:occupation">11</a>. Then $$\begin{aligned}
    \label{eq:prime-occupation}
     &\frac{r_{\mathrm{st}}^2}{\phi_\mathrm e(s_{\mathrm{st}})}
       \sum_{\mathfrak p}h(\mathfrak p)
            H_{i(\mathfrak p)}(r(\mathfrak p),s(\mathfrak p))\notag\\
     &\hspace{8mm}\longrightarrow
     \frac1{M_g}\sum_{i\in\{\mathrm e,\mathrm o\}}
       \int_0^\infty\int
        \frac{r^2\pi_i(s)}{\phi_i(s)}H_i(r,s)
           \mathbf 1_{s<r/\ell}\,ds\,\frac{dr}{r},
    \end{aligned}$$ where the inner domains are $s\geq2$ for $i=\mathrm e$ and $s\geq1$ for $i=\mathrm o$. The convergence is uniform in the starting ratios.

Along the standard prime paths with $x>2$ from the root, $$\label{eq:ref-residual-sum}
 \sum_{\mathfrak p}h(\mathfrak p)
       \left|R_{i(\mathfrak p)}^{(w)}
                   (r(\mathfrak p),b(\mathfrak p))\right|=o(B^{-2}).$$ The same assertion is uniform for lower starting ratios in $[1.99,2.3]$ and starting cutoffs $B$ tending to infinity with the parameters of <a href="#eq:parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:parameters">[eq:parameters]</a>.

On every fixed compact subset of their respective domains, $\delta_i^{(w)}$ converges uniformly to the continuous function $\delta_i$. Uniformly for sufficiently large $w$, $$\label{eq:ref-anomaly-decay}
 |\delta_i^{(w)}(r)|+|\delta_i(r)|
       \ll\exp(-c r\log(r+2)).$$

At the root of the reference tree, $$\label{eq:ref-margin}
 \frac{B^2P_\mathrm e(r_0,B)}{e^\gamma}
       \longrightarrow -a_\star+\frac{2I}{M_g}>0.02.$$ In particular, there is an absolute $c_L>0$ such that $B^2P_\mathrm e(r_0,B)\geq c_L$ for all sufficiently large $z$.

For the continuous reference polynomial, $$\label{eq:ref-local-cont}
 Q_\mathrm e(r,b)=\frac{f(r/b)}b+O(b^{-2})
 \qquad\left(b\longrightarrow\infty,\quad2\leq r/b\leq2.22\right),$$ uniformly in the displayed ratios. On every fixed cutoff window $[b_0,b_1]$ with $b_0$ sufficiently large, the corresponding discrete estimate is $$\label{eq:ref-local-prime}
 \frac{P_\mathrm e(r,b)}{V(b)}
        =f(r/b)+O(1/b)+o_{w\to\infty}(1),
 \qquad b\in[b_0,b_1],\quad2\leq r/b\leq2.22.$$ The constant in $O(1/b)$ is absolute, the last convergence is uniform on the fixed window, and either prime endpoint convention is allowed.

Fix $K$ and $K_*>K+10$. For every $\vartheta>0$ there are fixed constants $C_{\rm len}$ and $\alpha_{\min}>0$, followed by constants $\xi_0>0$ and $C_{\rm th}<\infty$, with the following properties. They are chosen before $z$ tends to infinity, and $C_{\rm th}$ is independent of $0<\xi\le\xi_0$. Put $$\label{eq:boxes-delta}
 \Delta=2C_{\rm len}\xi.$$ For each fixed $0<\xi\le\xi_0$ and sufficiently large $z$, there is a family $\mathcal G$ of regular included endpoints with $r_d\le K$ such that $$\label{eq:boxes-exception}
 \mathfrak m\bigl(\{d\text{ included}:r_d\le K\}\setminus\mathcal G\bigr)
 \le \frac{\vartheta+C_{\rm th}\Delta+o_\xi(1)}{B^2}.$$ The term $o_\xi(1)$ tends to zero after all fixed parameters, including $\xi$, have been chosen. The statement is uniform in the prescribed residue classes. The regular endpoints and their covering full boxes have these additional properties.*

1.  *Every regular endpoint has length at most $C_{\rm len}\log B$ and has an isolated position. Every used search bin, including its isolated bin, has multiplicity one. Every position satisfying $$x_j\le2K_*+3,\qquad r_{j-1}\le2K_*+5$$ also has multiplicity one. The isolated position precedes every edge supplied by Lemma <a href="#lem:edge-witness" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:edge-witness">25</a>.*



2.  *For every nonempty even prefix of a regular endpoint, $$\label{eq:boxes-threshold-clearance}
     r_j-\mathcal H(x_j)>10\Delta.$$ Moving prime choices anywhere within their fixed bins changes any prefix gap by at most $\Delta$, and changes a threshold expression $r_j-\mathcal H(x_j)$ by at most $\Delta+2h$. In particular the representative gaps and exponents of every regular box satisfy $$\label{eq:boxes-representative-safety}
     r_j^{\rm rep}\ge \mathcal H(x_j^{\rm rep})+3\Delta
     \quad\text{at every nonempty even prefix}.$$*


3.  *Let $\mathfrak B$ be the distinct full boxes containing at least one regular endpoint. There is a constant $C_K^{\rm box}$, depending only on a fixed enlargement of the endpoint compact range and the allowed starting ratios, such that $$\label{eq:boxes-expanded-mass}
     \sum_{\mathcal B\in\mathfrak B}\mathfrak m(\mathcal B)
           \le\frac{C_K^{\rm box}}{B^2}.$$ In particular this constant is independent of $\xi$, $\vartheta$, and the total number of boxes. Every tuple in these boxes is a standard path from initial gap $r_0+1$, ends at gap at most $K+2$ for that starting gap, and has its actual final $\log_wJ\ge3a_\star/4$.*




4.  *In a regular box, declare an edge a candidate if it is among the last $H_e=\lceil2K_*+10\rceil$ positions, is after the isolated position, has multiplicity one, and can satisfy the size bounds in <a href="#eq:boxes-witness" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:boxes-witness">[eq:boxes-witness]</a> for some tuple in the box. There are at most $H_e$ candidates, and every witness for a regular endpoint is among them. Throughout the whole box of each candidate, writing its parent as $d'=pq$ and its prime as $u$, one has $$\label{eq:boxes-candidate-slack}
     \log_w\frac{Y}{pq}\le2K_*+4,
     \qquad
     \log_w\frac{Y}{pqu}\ge\frac{3a_\star}{4}.$$ The bins for $p$ and $u$ have lower endpoints $R$ and $U$ satisfying $$\label{eq:boxes-prime-ranges}
     z^{\alpha_{\min}}\le R\le z^{\alpha_{\max}},
     \qquad w\le U\le w^{2K_*+4}.$$ The choices of $p$, $q$, $u$, and the descendants after this edge are independent under the full-box measure. All their prime factors are disjoint. If $S_q$ is the product of the bin upper endpoints for the factors of $q$, with their specified multiplicities, then $$\label{eq:boxes-sq}
     1\le\frac{S_q}{q}\le(1+\xi)^{C_{\rm len}\log B}.$$*

5.  *Every regular box has at least $c_7\log w$ search positions, for an absolute $c_7>0$ and sufficiently large $z$. These positions have distinct bins. All its nonsearch positions follow all its search positions.

Fix the compact range and $\varepsilon>0$. For every prescribed positive $\rho$, the inverse exception parameter and then the variance exception parameter can be chosen so that, for a fixed $C_s$ independent of sufficiently small fixed $\xi$, the following holds for all sufficiently large $z$. Consider the actual included regular compact endpoints covered by the full boxes of <a href="#prop:regular-boxes" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:regular-boxes">26</a>. Except for a set of these endpoints of total harmonic mass at most $\rho B^{-2}$, every such bad endpoint $$|N_d/\mu_d-1|>\varepsilon$$ has a witnessing candidate and a rational on that candidate’s fixed list for which the parent is structured, the isolated prime aligns, and $$\label{eq:variance-hard}
 Dq_{f,E}\le w^{C_s},
 \qquad |C_0|/R\le w^{C_s}.$$ For each such endpoint all its prime factors with exponent $x(t)>C_s$ align to this same rational, including the isolated prime. The rational satisfies the global bounds $$\label{eq:variance-global-height}
 D\le w^{C_s},
 \qquad |A|\le Yw^{C_s+2}.$$ The enlarged full boxes are used only to dominate measure: the assertion concerns the actual included endpoints, rather than every tuple in those enlarged boxes. The exceptional mass can be bounded before choosing $\xi$; only the eventual threshold on $z$ is allowed to depend on fixed $\xi$.

Fix $C_s$. Take $b_0$ sufficiently large in terms of $C_s$, and let $b_1>b_0$ be fixed. For every sufficiently small fixed $\xi>0$, the following holds for all sufficiently large $z$. Let $A/D$ be eligible in the sense of <a href="#eq:stops-eligible" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:stops-eligible">[eq:stops-eligible]</a>, let $P=w^b$ be a prime with $b\in[b_0-1,b_1]$, and let $q'$ be a squarefree product of primes at most $z$ whose least prime factor is $P$ and all of whose prime factors align to $A/D$. Let $\mathcal P$ be a search bin, none of whose primes divides $q'$. Suppose that, for every $p\in\mathcal P$, $$\label{eq:stops-length-exponents}
 J_p=\frac{Y}{pq'},\qquad 2.03\le\log_PJ_p\le2.19.$$ Define $T_p$ to count the integers $1\le n\le Y$ satisfying $Dn\equiv A\pmod{pq'}$ and avoiding the prescribed classes at every prime $t<P$. Then $$\label{eq:stops-all-primes-lower}
 \sum_{p\in\mathcal P}T_p
 \ge .39V_{\rm all}(P)\sum_{p\in\mathcal P}J_p.$$ There is also an absolute constant $C_U$, independent of $C_s,b_0,b_1$ and of sufficiently small fixed $\xi$, such that $$\label{eq:stops-individual-upper}
 T_p\le C_UJ_pV_{\rm all}(P)\qquad(p\in\mathcal P).$$ Both estimates are uniform in the eligible rational, the bin, the product $q'$, and the prescribed residue classes.

Uniformly over all subsets $E$ of the primes less than $P$, $$\label{eq:stops-euler-lower}
 \sum_{\substack{d_0\le P^{.95}\\t\mid d_0\Rightarrow t\in E}}
       \frac{W_E(d_0)}{d_0}
 \ge e^{-\gamma}(.94-o(1)).$$

Let $H,T_0,T_1$ be positive integers such that $H\ge2$, $T_0$ is squarefree, every prime divisor of $T_0$ divides $H$, and $(T_1,HT_0)=1$. Put $N=HT_0$. Let $C_0\in\mathbb Z$ satisfy $(C_0,H)=1$, and prescribe the residue pattern $$\label{eq:hyperbola-pattern}
 p_0\in(\mathbb Z/T_0\mathbb Z)^\times,\qquad k_0\in\mathbb Z/T_0\mathbb Z,\qquad
 p_1\in(\mathbb Z/T_1\mathbb Z)^\times,\qquad m_1\in\mathbb Z/T_1\mathbb Z.$$ The residue classes modulo $1$ have their usual unique interpretation. Let $I,J$ be bounded real intervals of lengths $X,Y_2\ge0$, with either endpoint independently included or excluded. For a positive integer $l$ with $(l,HT_1)=1$, let $C_l$ be the number of integer pairs $(p,m)$ satisfying $$\label{eq:hyperbola-counted-set}
\begin{gathered}
 p\in I,\qquad m\in J,\qquad l\mid p,\\
 p\equiv p_0\pmod{T_0},\qquad
 p\equiv p_1\pmod{T_1},\qquad
 m\equiv m_1\pmod{T_1},\\
 pm\equiv C_0+Hk_0\pmod N.
\end{gathered}$$ Here $p$ is an integer variable, without a primality condition. Uniformly in all these data, $$\label{eq:hyperbola-explicit-count}
 C_l=
 \frac{XY_2\varphi(N)}{lT_1^2N^2\varphi(T_0)}
 +O\left(
 \tau(N)^2\log^2(2N)\sqrt{NT_0}
 \left(1+\frac{X}{lN}+\frac{Y_2}{N}\right)
 \right),$$ with an absolute implied constant.*



*In particular, suppose that $R\to\infty$ and the data range uniformly over families satisfying $$\label{eq:hyperbola-application-range}
 R^{4/5}<H\le R\mathcal Z^{11},\qquad
 X\ll R,\qquad Y_2\le3H,\qquad
 \mathcal Z,T_0,T_1=R^{o(1)}.$$ Then, uniformly over all the patterns and intervals above and all squarefree $l\le R^{1/10}$ with $(l,HT_1)=1$, $$\label{eq:hyperbola-application-count}
 C_l=
 \frac{XY_2\varphi(N)}{lT_1^2N^2\varphi(T_0)}
 +O\bigl(R^{3/5+o(1)}\bigr).$$ Thus the same assertion holds with error $O(R^{7/10+o(1)})$. Uniformity in the last two assertions means that the upper bounds represented by $R^{o(1)}$ in <a href="#eq:hyperbola-application-range" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:hyperbola-application-range">[eq:hyperbola-application-range]</a> hold uniformly over the family; equivalently, for every $\delta>0$ the error in <a href="#eq:hyperbola-application-count" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:hyperbola-application-count">[eq:hyperbola-application-count]</a> is $O_\delta(R^{3/5+\delta})$ for sufficiently large $R$ in that family. No lower bound for $X/l$ is required.
